{"name":"Zest","hex":"#c6723b","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"zest","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The outer skin of a citrus fruit, used as a flavouring or garnish.","General vibrance of flavour."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To scrape the zest from a fruit.","To make more zesty."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/zest"}],"entries":[{"title":"Zest (ingredient)","blocks":[{"text":"Zest is a food ingredient that is prepared by scraping or cutting from the rind of unwaxed citrus fruits such as lemon, orange, citron, and lime. Zest is used to add flavor to many different types of food."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Zesting_an_orange.jpg/330px-Zesting_an_orange.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zest_(ingredient)","wikidata":"Q196819"},{"title":"Zester","blocks":[{"text":"A zester is a kitchen utensil for obtaining zest from lemons and other citrus fruit. A kitchen zester is approximately four inches (100 mm) long, with a handle and a curved metal end, the top of which is perforated with a row of round holes with sharpened rims. To operate, the zester is pressed with moderate force against the fruit and drawn across its peel. The rims cut the zest from the pith underneath. The zest is cut into ribbons, one drawn through each hole."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Zesting_an_orange.jpg/330px-Zesting_an_orange.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zester","wikidata":"Q196816"},{"title":"Zest (brand)","blocks":[{"text":"Zest is an American brand of soap and body wash owned by Sodalis USA for the United States, Canada, and Puerto Rico and by Unilever for the rest of the world. It was initially introduced by Procter & Gamble in 1955 with the slogan \"For the first time in your life, feel really clean.\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Zest_logo.svg/330px-Zest_logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zest_(brand)","wikidata":"Q8069689"},{"title":"Zest (positive psychology)","blocks":[{"text":"In positive psychology, zest is among the discrete strengths people possess. Having zest means treating life as an adventure and feeling motivated in challenging situations. People with zest display enthusiasm, excitement, and energy as they tackle life's tasks. Zest is a component of the virtue of courage in some positive psychology paradigms."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Everest_Peace_Project_-_Climbing_ladder_northcol_everest.jpg/330px-Everest_Peace_Project_-_Climbing_ladder_northcol_everest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zest_(positive_psychology)","wikidata":"Q8069690"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Juliennejern.JPG/500px-Juliennejern.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Juliennejern.JPG/1280px-Juliennejern.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Juliennejern.JPG/500px-Juliennejern.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Zesting a lime; the white mesocarp is visible under the green flavedo.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJuliennejern.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Removing_pith_%288449071157%29.jpg/500px-Removing_pith_%288449071157%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Removing_pith_%288449071157%